<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Overview</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    BloodConnect is a free, open-source mobile app, powered by Craftsmen, that connects people who
    need blood with donors. I worked with Shawon Setu on BloodConnect, in a collaboration between
    Craftsmen and UXBD.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Over six months, the two of us designed Phase 1 together: research, user stories, UI and error
    states. We worked to the client’s requirements, and the feature requests came from the
    stakeholder. A beta was released first.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">What we learned</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    We started from the stakeholder’s feature requests and added our own desk research, including a
    review of two existing blood-donation apps, Blood Donor App (Save Life Co) and BloodLine (Blood
    Bank App BD).
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    We also interviewed real donors and seekers, and wrote user stories for three types of user:
    Donor, Searcher and Organization.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/bloodconnect-research.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1200"
      height="900"
      alt="Diagram: stakeholder feature requests, desk research and interviews lead to user stories for Donor, Searcher and Organization"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>Three sources fed the user stories for each type of user.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">How the design came together</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    We mapped the flows with sitemaps and phased wireframes (Phase 1, Phase 2 and first release),
    then refined the UI across several versions up to handoff.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/bloodconnect-flow.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1200"
      height="900"
      alt="Illustrative recreation of the Phase 1 registration journey, from splash to settings, shown with demo content"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      The Phase 1 registration journey, from splash screen to settings (recreated with demo
      content).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p class="cs__p">Three decisions shaped the first-run experience:</p>
  <ol class="cs__p">
    <li>
      <strong>OTP via WhatsApp, with an email option.</strong> WhatsApp was the more feasible
      solution for the stakeholders.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>NID upload at registration.</strong> It is there to help stop people from using the
      platform for scams.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Privacy and notification settings.</strong> They give people the freedom to set up the
      platform the way they want.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/bloodconnect-decisions.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1200"
      height="900"
      alt="Illustrative recreation of the OTP, personal info with NID upload, and settings screens, shown with demo content"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      The three decisions on screen: OTP, NID upload and settings (recreated with demo content).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">What we handed off</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    Phase 1 covers getting people into the app safely and correctly. The login journey runs from the
    splash screen and a getting-started screen with account creation, login and guest access,
    through login, forgot password, OTP and set new password. The registration journey covers create
    account, OTP, set password, personal info and settings.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    We designed the error states alongside the happy path, including mandatory fields, a wrong OTP
    code and a password mismatch.
  </p>
  <figure class="cs__fig">
    <img
      src="{{ '/assets/images/projects/bloodconnect-errors.webp' | rel }}"
      width="1200"
      height="900"
      alt="Illustrative recreation of three error states: mandatory fields, wrong OTP code and password mismatch, shown with demo content"
      loading="lazy"
      decoding="async"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Error states designed alongside the happy path (recreated with demo content).
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">Outcome</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    The design was handed off on 5 October 2024, and a beta is available on Google Play.
  </p>
  <p class="cs__p">
    As of 2 November 2026, the public
    <a
      href="https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=net.bloodconnect.app"
      target="_blank"
      rel="noopener"
      >Google Play listing<span class="sr-only"> (opens in a new tab)</span></a
    >
    shows 500+ downloads and a 4.8 rating from 5 reviews. These are figures for the app as a whole,
    not measurements of my design.
  </p>
</section>
<section class="cs__sec">
  <h2 class="cs__h">What I learned</h2>
  <p class="cs__p">
    This project taught me how to build a user journey and how to think like a user while designing
    a product. It also taught me how to create a happy path, and how to apply UI aesthetics,
    hierarchy and usability.
  </p>
</section>
